#Notlob Vids

Short animations about notlob, written in notlob. Each video is the
output of a program. What a video shows is not drawn by hand or typed
in: source listings are coloured by notlob's own parser, claim results
come from running the claims, measurements are taken when the video is
rendered, and the name-graph is the one notlob exports. The claims in
these modules check the parts of the design that can be checked without
watching a render -- layout, timing, coverage, and that what is shown
still matches the projects it is taken from.

The first video is a teaser of thirty seconds, built around one module
of the pn-chomper game. #Storyboard sets out its beats, their lengths
and captions as data. #Teaser draws each beat and joins them.

The rest is shared, and would serve another video as well. #Style holds
the frame, the palette and the captions. #Highlight turns notlob source
into a coloured listing. #Graph decides where every name in a project's
name-graph sits. #Evidence runs the real things -- a project's claims,
a query, its code -- and returns what happened. #Board draws a Petri net
the way pn-chomper's own renderer does. #Sound synthesises a sound track
from the same timings the pictures use, though the teaser is at present
rendered silent. #Review makes contact sheets of rendered videos, so a
render can be checked from a few of its frames.

Rendering uses manim. The environment is declared in pyproject.toml and
rebuilt with run.sh, which also provides the test, render and frames
entry points.

